---
// TASK 2: My top 10 favourites
// Create a JavaScript object here with 10 entries.
// Each key can be a favourite's name and its value a reason you like it.
// Keep entries in your preferred order, using names rather than numeric keys.
//
// Object.entries(yourObject) makes an array of [key, value] pairs.
// Use .map(([name, reason], index) => ...) in the HTML below.
// Indexes start at 0, so index + 1 gives you ranks 1 to 10.
//
// LATER, TASK 4: Move your data to src/data/favourites.json and import it here.
// Replace your local object with the shared data and link each name to its page.
// See README.md for the new data shape and a link hint.

import favourites from "../data/favourites.json";
---

<!--
  Build your favourites section here.
  1. Add a <section> and a heading (<h2>).
  2. Use an ordered list (<ol>) for your ranking.
  3. Use Object.entries(...).map(...) to generate one <li> per favourite.
  4. Display each name, reason, and its rank using index + 1.

  This generates HTML at build time; no browser script is needed yet.
  See README.md for a smaller example if you get stuck.
-->

<div>
	<h2>My Top 10 Favourite Games</h2>
	<p>(In the order I obtained and first played them)</p>

	<ol>
		{Object.entries(favourites).map(([slug, favourite], index) => (
			<li value={index + 1}>
				<h3>{favourite.name}</h3>
				<p>{favourite.reason}</p>
				<a href={`/favourites/${slug}/`}>More about {favourite.name}</a>
			</li>
		))}
	</ol>
</div>
